escribing, and it is not "lawyer who is good with computers". It is <strong>legal quant</strong>: a lawyer who builds their own tooling to practise law, rather than waiting for a vendor to ship it. The distinction that matters to us: plenty of lawyers now use AI. A legal quant builds with it. They look at a workflow and ask how to redesign it from first principles to get the best possible outcome, rather than making the existing process incrementally faster.</p><p><strong>A note on how we are weighting this role.</strong> Three capabilities make someone exceptional here: commercial instinct, legal depth, and technical ability to build. The ideal candidate has all three. We know that person is rare, and we are not going to pretend otherwise by writing a wish list and hoping. So for this role we weight the technical evidence most heavily, because it is the hardest of the three to teach on the job.</p><p>If you believe you genuinely combine all three, we want to talk to you regardless of how neatly your CV matches the bullets below. Say so in your note and tell us what you have built and what you have sold.</p><p><strong>1. Technical track record.</strong> You have built things and they exist and ideally you have <strong>published</strong> some of it. You are fluent with LLMs the way a practitioner is fluent, not a spectator: you know how context windows fail you or why a prompt stopped working. When something breaks you go into the system rather than around it. Scripting, Git, and APIs are a strong plus.</p><p><strong>2. Legal foundation.</strong> A law degree with a commercial-contracts focus: German-qualified (Bachelor, Master, or Staatsexamen) or an equivalent qualification from another jurisdiction. <strong>No bar admission required, no second Staatsexamen required.</strong> What we need: you have worked hands-on with German commercial contracts, drafting, reviewing, and negotiating them, and you can hold a conversation with a General Counsel as a peer.</p><p><strong>3. Commercial and entrepreneurial track record.</strong> You have carried commercial responsibility for something of your own: a freelance client base, a legal tech product, or a company you started. You know what it takes to win a client from a standing start and why "they did not understand the value" is never the customer's fault. If instead you have supported enterprise sales teams in demos, pilots, and expert calls, tell us what you changed about the outcome.</p><p>You prioritise by impact, you are comfortable deciding without permission, and you would rather ship something imperfect this week than something elegant next quarter.</p><p><strong>4. Language.</strong> Excellent German and English. German is non-negotiable: our customers are DACH legal teams and the nuance lives in their language. You can run a room of legal buyers, ask the sharp question, and write a specification precise enough to build from. Other EU languages are a plus.</p></p><br><strong>Why us?</strong><p><ul><li><strong>Impact over hype.</strong> You define a new discipline at the intersection of law, product, and AI. Your work directly shapes how thousands of legal professionals will operate.</li><li><strong>No silos.</strong> You sit between Product, Engineering, and the customer, with a direct line to all three. The better argument wins, not the higher title.</li><li><strong>We invest in you.</strong> The enterprise motion (multi-stakeholder pilots, procurement cycles, security reviews) is teachable, and we teach it: side by side in real customer calls from week one.</li><li><strong>Real ownership.</strong> Competitive salary and employee stock options (ESOP).</li><li><strong>Build in public.</strong> We want you to keep publishing what you build, because it makes you better and it makes us visible.</li><li><strong>Offices in Berlin, Leipzig, and Zurich</strong> with a flexible hybrid setup. Regular apéros, lunches, and hiking trips as we enjoy the journey together.</li></ul></p><br>